```
Now that you have created a benign program, you can examine its behavior to learn about viruses.

A virus may spread from one computer to another by attaching itself to an email message or by infecting a program that is downloaded from the Internet. When a user runs the infected email or program, the virus spreads to the user's computer.

To spread, a virus must be able to replicate itself and attach itself to other files. You can simulate this behavior by creating a copy of your benign program and modifying it to spread.

To create a copy of your benign program, right-click the project in Solution Explorer and select Copy. Then, right-click in the Solution Explorer and select Paste. Visual Studio will create a new project with the same name as the original project.

Next, open the new project in the Solution Explorer. You will see that the code is the same as the original project. You can modify the code to spread.

